.TH SSMCP 1 "2026-11-16" "ssmcp 0.4.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ssmcp \- expose a local shell and installed coding harnesses through an SSH-authenticated MCP gateway
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ssmcp
.RB [ \-\-name " name" ]
.RB [ \-\-title " title" ]
.RB [ \-\-allow " username@github[,username@github...]" ]
.RB [ \-F " ssh_config" ]
.RB [ \-i " identity" ]
.RB [ \-p " port" ]
.RB [ \-\-shell " path" ]
.I [user@]host
.PP
.B ssmcp
.B \-\-list-tools
.SH DESCRIPTION
ssmcp authenticates to OpenSSH using a public key and starts one local shell/PTY client. Coding harnesses installed on the client are discovered automatically and their actual native tools are registered in the same code_mode catalog. Supported harness integrations include Codex App Server, Claude Code MCP, Pi RPC and OpenCode v2 API.
.PP
The server assigns each connection a random client ID. With \-\-allow, it returns the global HTTPS /mcp URL for GitHub identity authorization. Without \-\-allow, it returns the SSH account's /username URL and a one-time login code.
.PP
One JSON object containing url, client_id, name, type, hostname and cwd, plus title when set, is written to standard output. The type is always shell. GitHub mode adds auth:"github" and the canonical allow list; login-code mode adds login_code and login_expires_in. Live activity and diagnostics use standard error. The cwd field is the startup directory.
.PP
The external MCP catalog contains exactly two tools: list and code_mode. list discovers clients and their merged native tool catalog. code_mode runs JavaScript that calls a selected capability over SSH. The client uses one shell entry point. Remove obsolete \-t, \-\-type and \-\-backend options when migrating an older command.
.PP
Keep the program running while its client is in use. Ctrl-C or SIGTERM closes the SSH connection and stops its harnesses and background jobs. Login-code grants issued by that connection are revoked. GitHub identity grants remain subject to their own lifetimes and each currently connected client's allow policy.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-name " name"
Set the client display name. The default combines hostname and process ID. Names are limited to 128 UTF-8 bytes and may not contain control characters. Duplicate names are allowed; use the server-generated client ID to select an unambiguous target.
.TP
.BI \-\-allow " username@github[,username@github...]"
Allow verified GitHub users to access this connection. Repeat this option or separate entries with commas. At most 32 distinct users are accepted; case is normalized and duplicates are removed. Usernames contain 1 to 39 ASCII letters or digits with optional single hyphens between them.
.PP
This option requires an SSH destination and cannot accompany \-\-list-tools. No GitHub login is performed in the CLI; existing SSH keys and configuration remain in use. The server must support and configure GitHub OAuth.
.TP
.BI \-\-title " title"
Set the session task shown in client discovery, code_mode's clients snapshot
and connection output. Accepts Unicode and spaces, with at most 512 UTF-8 bytes.
Empty, whitespace-only and control-character titles are rejected.
The title is independent of the display name and client ID; it does not execute
commands or change access policy. When omitted, no title field is sent.
A nonempty title requires a server that supports this metadata field.
.TP
.BI \-F " file"
Use a separate OpenSSH client configuration file.
.TP
.BI \-i " file"
Use the specified private key. ssh-agent is also supported.
.TP
.BI \-p " port"
Connect to the specified SSH port.
.TP
.BI \-\-shell " path"
Run the specified absolute shell path in the local pseudo-terminal. The default is the absolute path in SHELL, or /bin/sh.
.TP
.B \-\-list-tools
Probe local providers, print the discovered capability catalog and shut them down. Does not connect to SSH and does not accept user@host.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print usage information and exit.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH MCP TOOLS
.TP
.B list
With no arguments, return online client IDs, names, shell type, hostnames and startup directories that the current authorization may access. With client_id, return that client's merged native tool names, descriptions and schemas. Pass a returned nextCursor as cursor to read another capability page.
.TP
.B code_mode
Accept a code string and optional timeout_ms. JavaScript has clients, await call(clientId, toolName, arguments), await tools(clientId, cursor), Promise.all, print, console.log and top-level return. Calls return complete native MCP results, including isError and structuredContent. Inspect isError even when the JavaScript promise resolves.
.PP
The server starts a system-installed Node.js process inside bubblewrap for each
request. Node.js 18 or newer, bubblewrap 0.8 or newer and util-linux prlimit are
server dependencies managed by the operating system package manager; no npm package is needed. A client-only
ssmcp installation does not require these server dependencies. The public
JavaScript API includes clients, call, tools, logging, reload, outputs.read/stat/cleanup,
fs, context, skills, agents, rsync/sync, jobs, ports and resources. Short native
results are returned unchanged. Long backend output is replaced by artifact
metadata; inspect spilled and use outputs.read. PTY artifacts contain raw terminal
bytes; other oversized backend results contain the saved MCP result JSON.
Native backend calls execute
on the selected client with that local user's permissions. Runtime state is
fresh for each request; backend sessions remain alive.
.PP
The bridge script is installed at PREFIX/libexec/ssmcp/code-mode.js, normally
/usr/local/libexec/ssmcp/code-mode.js. The server environment variable
SSMCP_CODE_MODE_RUNNER may select an absolute script path. Missing dependencies
or unavailable namespace isolation cause code_mode to fail; list remains usable.
.PP
Timeout stops orchestration, but an already-started native action may continue.
Inspect native state before retrying a mutating action. Retain output cursors
and agent session IDs in the calling application for subsequent requests.
Native capabilities include terminal.*, ssmcp.exec.*, ssmcp.fs.*, ssmcp.skills.*, and tools exposed by installed harnesses. Harness namespaces are codex.*, cc.*, pi.* and opencode.*. Only actually discovered capabilities are registered.
.SH EXECUTION
.TP
.B ssmcp.exec.start
Starts an independent argv process in the background and returns a job ID. stdout and stderr have separate cursors. Observe tasks with ssmcp.exec.list, ssmcp.exec.status, ssmcp.exec.wait, ssmcp.exec.read and ssmcp.exec.events; stop a task with ssmcp.exec.cancel. Use any returned output artifact metadata to read retained bytes.
.TP
.B ssmcp.fs.write
Atomically writes a workspace-relative file. Inspect the result and available audit events for completion and observable changes. File paths in tool arguments alone do not establish successful reads or writes. Skills discovered by list/view and documents read by skills.read are distinct from a native skill invocation. External operations absent from the native event interface are outside the observable audit scope.
.PP
Independent jobs do not share the interactive PTY. code_mode supports Promise.all for independent calls. Check each task's final state and exit code; a start acknowledgement only confirms that the process was launched.
.PP
Native permissions and approvals remain enforced by the provider. MCP does not guarantee that the caller's model identity is exposed; ssmcp does not infer it.
.SH AUTHENTICATION
The server resolves allowed GitHub usernames to stable user IDs when each
connection attaches. Discovery and calls check that connection's IDs. A rename
does not change an existing connection's identity match; reconnecting resolves
the startup usernames again. Update \-\-allow after a username change. No
cross-connection username pin is persisted. An authorized MCP client may see
an empty list until a matching connection comes online.
.PP
Without \-\-allow, add the returned /username URL and enter login_code on the
OAuth page. This grant covers only clients without an allow list under that SSH
account and depends on its issuing connection. It cannot access any client
started with \-\-allow, even under the same SSH account.
.PP
Both modes complete Authorization Code with S256 PKCE and use ssmcp-issued
access tokens as Bearer credentials. GitHub tokens are used to read the verified
identity and are not retained. Repository and email scopes are not requested.
.PP
The destination may be a Host alias from ~/.ssh/config. OpenSSH resolves its
HostName, User, Port, IdentityFile, IdentityAgent and ProxyJump settings. An
explicit user@host overrides the configured User. Use \-F for a separate
configuration. Local ssh-agent authentication works without agent forwarding;
ssmcp disables forwarding, connection multiplexing and remote TTY allocation.
.PP
Public keys are managed by the server's OpenSSH authorized_keys policy. A key
already accepted for that account can be reused. On a ForceCommand-restricted
ssmcp account, update authorized_keys through a separate administrator account
or server console; ssh-copy-id and SFTP cannot execute their normal operations
through the fixed bridge command. Append the new public key, test a second
connection, then remove only the old key. Removing a key affects new SSH
authentication; close an existing connection to revoke its OAuth grants.
.PP
The login code expires 1800 seconds after issue and is consumed by one successful
authorization. login_expires_in subtracts backend startup time. In 0.4.0, access
tokens default to at most 8 hours. Refresh tokens rotate on successful use and
have a default 30-day idle period that slides forward on each refresh, bounded
by a 90-day authorization-family lifetime measured from initial authorization.
Client registrations and secrets default to 365 days. Server operators can
configure these four lifetimes; tokens are also bounded by remaining grant and
registration lifetimes.
.PP
Registration without grant_types provisions both authorization_code and
refresh_token. Explicitly registering only authorization_code does not issue
a refresh token. The MCP client must implement renewal and save the latest
rotated refresh token. Before expiry, or after a 401 challenge with
error="invalid_token", it requests grant_type=refresh_token with the current
refresh token, original resource URL and registered client authentication.
It saves the new token pair and retries with the new access token. Serialize
refreshes for one grant; reusing a consumed refresh token revokes the grant.
.PP
GitHub grants are bound to the verified identity, resource and OAuth client,
without an issuing SSH connection. They may access current and future clients
whose allow lists match that identity. Login-code grants are additionally bound
to their issuing SSH connection and exclude all allow-listed clients. Closing
that source revokes its access and refresh tokens. Reconnect to obtain a new
login code when a fresh login-code authorization is needed.
.PP
Reopening an MCP application can reuse its saved, valid OAuth tokens. GitHub
grants survive local client reconnects, while session IDs and backends change;
the new connection must explicitly allow the identity. Login-code grants require
their source connection to remain alive. Restarting ssmcpd clears connections and active authorizations, so both modes require a new login. With DCR persistence enabled, unexpired client registrations remain available. Access-token expiry can
be handled by silent refresh. Expired, revoked or replayed refresh tokens produce
invalid_grant; sign in again using the selected mode. Updating source alone does
not upgrade the running daemon.
.PP
Native agent permissions, request IDs and approval responses remain explicit.
ssmcp does not automatically answer native requests. Install and configure optional harnesses on the client; the gateway does not need those harnesses installed.
.SH CONTEXT AND TEXT FILES
Every backend provides native skill metadata, instruction views and UTF-8 file
primitives through code_mode. Use context.roots, skills.list/read/view,
agents.list/read/resolve and fs.readText/writeText/editText.
The workspace root is the startup directory. Repeat
.B --context-root PATH
to add up to eight existing read-only context directories. Select their returned
root IDs explicitly; these grants do not extend ordinary file writes.
Skill views index name and description frontmatter. Full documents are read on
demand. Instruction views follow the selected root to a target and preserve
source paths and contents. No model is started by these operations.

.SH CLIENT RELOAD
After installing a replacement at the original client entry point, use
.B await reload(clientId)
inside code_mode. The native equivalent is
.BR "call(clientId, \(dqssmcp.client.reload\(dq, {})" .
The request uses the existing client authorization and accepts no arguments.
Reload is refused while other tool requests are in flight or the entry point
is unavailable. On acceptance it returns accepted:true and status:restarting,
waits for the server acknowledgement, stops the old connection's background
commands, harnesses and forwards, and re-executes the installed entry point.
.PP
Startup arguments, working directory and environment are preserved, including
title and allow policy. Reload does not download, build or install an update.
Use list again to confirm reconnection and select the new client ID by title,
name and cwd. A restarting acknowledgement is not proof of reconnection.
Valid GitHub identity grants can continue accessing the matching new connection;
login-code mode requires a fresh OAuth login. Upgrade both client and server
before using reload. Old clients without the reload tool require a manual
restart for their first upgrade.

.SH LONG OUTPUT
Output exceeding 32 KiB or 500 lines is stored on the client under
.IR /tmp/ssmcp/<uid>/output-<id>.log .
The result carries metadata instead of the full output: ID, path, SHA-256,
byte and line counts, observed_bytes, complete and expires_at_unix.
The digest covers the first N bytes at that snapshot, where N is the bytes field.
Lines count newline
records and a final nonempty unterminated record. Complete describes capture
integrity, not whether the command succeeded.
.PP
A PTY artifact
covers the whole terminal session's merged raw stream, including terminal control
bytes. Other oversized native results are stored as the MCP result JSON received
by ssmcp. Output already truncated by a native harness cannot be recovered.
.PP
Use outputs.read(clientId,{id,offset,max_bytes}) for a chunk, with byte offsets,
16 KiB by default and 32 KiB maximum. Reuse next_offset. The result contains text
and exact data_base64; its SHA-256 covers only the returned chunk. EOF refers to
the current size and does not mean an active capture has finished.
Use outputs.stat(clientId,{id}) for current metadata and a whole-file snapshot
digest. The native tools are ssmcp.output.read and ssmcp.output.stat.
.PP
Retention is 24 hours from the latest write or completion. Completed artifacts
are limited to 512 MiB in total, with oldest files evicted first. Each artifact
is limited to 256 MiB; exceeding it or failing to write marks an existing saved
prefix incomplete and supplies an error. If artifact creation itself fails,
the operation reports an error and may have no readable artifact.
Active captures hold file locks and are skipped
by cleanup, so their bytes may temporarily exceed the completed-file quota.
Unrelated files are preserved.
.PP
Cleanup runs at output-store startup, artifact creation and every 60 seconds
while the client runs. outputs.cleanup(clientId), or the native
ssmcp.output.cleanup tool with empty arguments, runs the same policy immediately
and returns removed and retained counts. It does not unconditionally empty the
directory. Cleanup resumes at next startup if all clients have exited.
The local --max-inline-output display setting does not change these agent-facing
output thresholds.

.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ssmcp --name build-shell user@host
ssmcp --name build-shell my-server
ssmcp --name build-shell --title "Fix the client build" my-server
ssmcp --allow octocat@github --name build-shell my-server
ssmcp --allow alice@github,bob@github --allow carol@github my-server
ssmcp --list-tools
.fi
.SH FILES
The bridge script is installed at PREFIX/libexec/ssmcp/code-mode.js, normally /usr/local/libexec/ssmcp/code-mode.js. The server environment variable SSMCP_CODE_MODE_RUNNER may select an absolute script path.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ssh (1),
.BR ssh_config (5),
.BR ssmcpd (8)

.SH LOG OUTPUT
.TP
.B --log-format pretty|pretty-verbose|jsonl
Select compact colored summaries, expanded events, or one JSON event per line.
.TP
.B --no-color
Disable ANSI colors. NO_COLOR and redirected stderr also disable them.
.TP
.B --verbose
Alias for --log-format pretty-verbose.
.TP
.B --raw-events
Alias for --log-format jsonl. --raw remains supported.
.TP
.B --ui-config FILE
Load local TOML display settings. Default: ~/.config/ssmcp/config.toml.
.TP
.B --theme minimal|codex-dark|nord|solarized
Select the terminal palette.
.TP
.B --timestamps
Include UTC timestamps on event headings.
.TP
.B --max-inline-output N
Show at most N lines per buffered output block. Default: 12.
